  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From 2001 to 2020, Nintendo, Sony, and Microsoft \ntraded blows across generations in an endless  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5360,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "battle of hardware and software. The console \nwars followed the same business pattern that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10160,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rules nearly all consumer electronics, where \nwinners rarely stay on top for long. The leader of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15040,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one gaming generation would almost always stumble \nin the next, brought down by hubris, disruption,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19680,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or both. Whatever market share they lost would \nimmediately get captured by the underdog,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24320,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who would seize the opening to rise to the \ntop, only to eventually stumble themselves. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29520,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The early 2000s was ruled by Sony, who dominated \nwith the PS2. Their success attracted Microsoft,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36000,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who spent billions to buy a seat at the table \nand kickstarted an arms race over graphics and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40880,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "power. This forced Nintendo to retreat to the \ncasual, non-gamer market. By the mid-2000s,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the leaderboard had flipped. The Xbox 360 \nbirthed cover-based shooters like Gears of War,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51680,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "defined the modern multiplayer experience with \nHalo 2 and Call of Duty, and elevated video  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55520,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "games into art with Bioshock. Sony countered \nwith PS3 exclusives like Uncharted and God of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61440,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "War 3 but Microsoft would ultimately win the \nhype and headlines in this generation. Yet the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66560,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "order would reverse once more in the mid-2010s \nwith Microsoft falling into the trap of its own  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71600,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "success and Sony taking back its top spot.\nNow a decade later, the console wars have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78320,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ended in the 2020s - not with a bang, but with \na whimper. As a distant third in hardware sales  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82720,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for two consecutive generations - behind the PS5, \nthe original Switch, and soon-to-be Switch 2, the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Xbox is barely part of the conversation. These \ndays, there aren’t even enough console owners  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for Microsoft to sell to. As a result, the iconic \nfranchises that made Xbox so special in the first  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95680,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "place have been devalued to the point that they \nmust be ported to the Playstation and Switch to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "turn a profit. Microsoft has reduced Xbox to \na rent-seeking, platform-agnostic publisher,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105280,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but rather than upholding the creativity, quality, \nand prestige that made people loyal to the Xbox in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110800,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the first place, they’ve done the exact opposite \n- shuttering studios left and right, pushing out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115760,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unfinished slop to fill subscriptions, shoving \nmicrotransactions at every turn, and harvesting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121120,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "engagement to appease shareholders. In this Modern \nMBA episode, we’ll cover the 4 eras of Xbox and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how the rise and fall of the console is a timeless \ncase study on politics, monetization, and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133200,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dangers that arise when competition disappears.\nFrom 2001 to 2004, the Xbox was in its first  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "era - raw, unproven, but fueled by unmatched \ncapital and corporate aggression. This was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145920,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the period of the dot-com boom. Microsoft was \nunder siege on multiple fronts - first was the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151520,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "U.S. government who threatened to break \nup the company under antitrust concerns,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155520,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then the emergence of the Internet which offered \nconsumers and developers far more exciting,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159920,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "engaging experiences online than the Windows \ndesktop ever could, and finally, the rise of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165280,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "open-source software like Linux. While Microsoft \nwould ultimately emerge battered but victorious,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170880,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these existential threats all revealed the \nfragility of Windows in the era of the public  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175760,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Internet. As the world shifted away from operating \nsystems and towards applications and browsers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "any non-Windows device that could get consumers \nonline meant one less Windows license,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186080,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one less developer in the Win32 ecosystem, and \nless money for Bill Gates and Steve Ballmer. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the time, Sony was surging with the Playstation \n2. Microsoft cared less about gamers and more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197280,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about stopping the PS2. In their eyes, the PS2 \nwas not a console, but instead a dangerous,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203120,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "non-Windows computer spreading through living \nrooms worldwide. If Sony was to add Linux,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208240,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a keyboard and mouse, and a web browser, this \nharmless Japanese gaming toy and DVD player  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could quickly transform into a low-cost Windows \nreplacement. It didn’t help that Sony marketed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218640,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the PS2 in the same way, asserting their CPUs to \nbe more powerful than Intel’s. From this lens, the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Xbox was a defensive play. Microsoft was willing \nto lose billions to make sure Sony wouldn’t own  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230400,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the living room. They followed the same playbook \nthey had used to conquer enterprises and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234560,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "datacenters in prior decades. The first Xbox was \na tank, with an industry-first 8GB hard drive when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241280,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the PS2 only supported 8MB memory cards, a custom \nNVIDIA processor that rendered Dolby 5.1 sound  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247760,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in real-time, allowing players to pinpoint enemy \nfootsteps based on their in-game direction, and a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253120,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "built-in Ethernet port. Compared to the GameCube \nand PS2 which were designed and positioned as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258400,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "toys, the Xbox was built like a PC with the most \npowerful internals on the market. But all this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264080,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "power would be pointless without games that could \ntake advantage of all this hardware. In the run-up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268960,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to launch, Microsoft poached Bungie from under \nApple’s nose in search of a platform-defining hit.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274320,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Halo exceeded all expectations as a launch-day \nexclusive, genre-defining title, and technical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280160,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "showcase with sprawling environments, \nreflective textures, water ripples,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284880,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ultra-smart enemies, and fast-paced multiplayer.\nTo drive adoption, the Xbox was priced the same  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290400,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as the PS2, which had already been on the market \nfor over a year. But since the Xbox was made from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295680,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "off the shelf Intel and NVIDIA components, \nMicrosoft lost $125 on every console sold.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302000,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In contrast, the PS2 and GameCube were not as \naggressive of loss leaders. Sony made their own  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307920,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "CPUs and GPUs. They owned the fabs and iterated \non their in-house components fast enough to turn  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312640,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a profit on every PS2 sold by the mid-2000s. \nMeanwhile, Nintendo architected its own chips  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and designed the GameCube to break-even despite \nselling for less. Gaming consoles are based on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324240,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the classic razor-blade business model, where \ncompanies generally sell hardware at cost or at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329040,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "loss and make their money back on the refills \nand add-ons that make the device usable in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the first place. With 6 times the units sold, \nthe largest player base, the biggest library,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339200,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a $7-10 royalty off every third-party game, \nand a slim profit on every PS2 Slim sold - the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Playstation division in this era was Sony’s \ncrown jewel. On the other hand, Nintendo sold  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350960,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fewer Gamecubes than Xbox, but turned a profit as \nsoon as the player bought a single game. Because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nintendo develops and publishes a large share of \nits own titles, the company consistently captures  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361120,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the highest profit margins - regardless of unit \nsales or player base. Ultimately, Nintendo was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366720,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not focused on winning the living room. Yet the \nGamecube had still been a disappointment given  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370880,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how many N64 players were lost to the PS2. \nThe real money for them was in handhelds.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376320,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Game Boy Advance was cheaper to make, more \nprofitable, and delivered the same high-margin  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381280,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first-party sales and third-party royalties across \na player base that was just as big as the PS2. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387440,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Under this lens, Microsoft was the outlier. \nThey had bought their way to performance and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391360,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in doing so, earned the worst unit economics. \nWith every console sold at a three-figure loss,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396720,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Microsoft needed Xbox players to spend far more \nthan PS2 and Gamecube players just to break-even.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402880,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As a result, they charged the most for controllers \nand accessories from the get-go to reduce this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deficit as quickly as possible. Still, everyone \nknew that software would be the only way to black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413040,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but this model only works if you have the games \nthat people want to play. If you don’t have the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417440,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "games, you don’t have the players. If you don’t \nhave the players, you don’t have the developers.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421600,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if you don’t have the developers, you don’t \nget royalties. Halo alone would never be enough  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426080,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to sustain the business. If no one else was \ngoing to make games for Xbox, then Microsoft  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430640,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would just have to do it themselves. Thus, \nthe only way they could make money in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434720,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "future was to simply spend more of it today.\nAs the new kid, Microsoft lacked the decades  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of brand loyalty and legendary franchises \nthat Nintendo and Sony had like Zelda, Mario,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Metal Gear Solid, and Final Fantasy. With the \nlarger player base, the vast majority of studios  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "naturally prioritized the PS2. It didn’t help that \nSony immediately went on the offensive to starve  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455760,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Xbox of the third-party titles it desperately \nneeded by signing Rockstar, Capcom, Namco,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460400,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Disney into exclusivity windows. It would \nbe years before Grand Theft Auto, Devil May Cry,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466000,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Kingdom Hearts were ported to Xbox and anyone \nwho wanted to play these games while they were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470800,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "popular could only do so on a PS2. Microsoft had \nno choice but to dig deeper into its warchest,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476960,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "funneling hundreds of millions into internal \ntitles like MechAssault contracting external  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481760,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "studios for Splinter Cell, Knights of the \nOld Republic, Morrowind, Fable, Ninja Gaiden,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and acquiring proven developers like Rare. These \ngames were made both for Americans seeking mature,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492560,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Western experiences and to showcase all that the \nhardware could accomplish. It was a chicken and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497360,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "egg conundrum for what was still a defensive \nventure. Microsoft had already sunk billions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502240,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just getting to market only to lose hundreds \non every Xbox sold and now found itself forced  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506960,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to make games under the hope that some of these \nexclusives would move the needle for gamers and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "developers. As with every creative endeavor, \nevery game was a coin flip that took years to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516480,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get to market. Since Microsoft was footing most of \nthe bill, every flop added to an already massive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521760,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deficit. While the company had no choice but to \nkeep its foot on the gas on game development,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526400,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they quickly bet the future of Xbox on a \ndifferent kind of software - one that neither  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530640,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sony nor Nintendo had ever taken seriously.\nAt $49.99 per year, Xbox Live elevated online  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537680,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gaming. Multiplayer on the PC and PS2 was a \nWild West. Players manually scrolled through  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543600,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "servers hosted by randoms, signed into a different \naccount for every game, and troubleshooted lag on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every session. Xbox Live standardized connection, \nmatchmaking, performance, and social features to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unify players across games into a single gated \ncommunity. Every player got a single identity,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559920,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "friends list, dashboard, and would be matched \ninto games by their connection and skill level.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564800,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Xbox Live required broadband Internet, which was \na radical mandate in an era when 80% of American  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570880,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "households were still on dial-up. At the same \ntime, players couldn’t just plug in an Ethernet  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575520,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cable, instead, they had to buy the Starter Kit, \nwhich included headsets. Microsoft envisioned  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580160,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "voice chat as the future and giving away headsets \nmeant that no one would have the excuse of not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584800,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "having the gear to talk in a lobby. Thus, Xbox \nLive wasn’t just about better online play, it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590240,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was about introducing network effects that would \nturn one-time customers into lifetime subscribers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595120,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and lock them in tighter than any individual game \never could. It was horizontal integration that no  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600480,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other console manufacturer had ever attempted.\nUltimately, Microsoft was the only company  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605280,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the world who had the expertise and \nconviction to execute on such a definitive,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609680,
    "dDurationMs": 1407,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "commercialized vision. It was a radical step \nof horizontal integration that no other console  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611087,
    "dDurationMs": 2833,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "manufacturer had ever attempted. All the insight \nthat Microsoft had picked up in the 90s building  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613920,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chat lobbies and matchmaking for millions of \nPC gamers was poured into Xbox Live. This kind  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619280,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of peer-to-peer software had been Microsoft’s \nbread and butter for decades. Xbox Live ran on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624320,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same datacenters that powered Microsoft’s \nenterprise empire. Its code, architecture,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629280,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and logic was adapted from other services like \nMSN Messenger, Hotmail, and Passport. In contrast,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635680,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sony and Nintendo both left online play up to \nindividual developers both in inclusion and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "implementation. They worried that asking consumers \nto pay for online play would only hurt console  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645680,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sales. Meanwhile, Microsoft was willing to bet \nthat people would be willing to pay for quality  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650480,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and convenience, so long as the experience \nwas curated and governed by the platform. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655200,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They would be proven right just 2 years later \nwith Halo 2. By 2005, Xbox Live had reached  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660960,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over a million subscribers. Subscription \nrevenue brought in much-needed cash flow,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but Xbox Live still added more to the bill. \nWhenever Sony discounted the PS2, Microsoft  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670640,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had to price match, which worsened their already \nterrible unit economics. Thus, while the overall  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676000,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Xbox business grew rapidly through this era, its \ngrowth was quickly offset by costs. The division  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681680,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was still losing over a billion dollars every \nyear even though the numbers of consoles sold  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685840,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and games on the platform had grown substantially. \nAt this point, the Xbox was only in its 3rd year.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691840,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The average console lifespan was 5-6. While there \nwas growth on every other front, there was no way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697200,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out of the console’s core unit economics. It \nwas too dangerous to endure another 2-3 years  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702320,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of this generation for the sake of convention. \nMicrosoft had no choice but to fast-track the next  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "generation to get out of its deals with NVIDIA and \nIntel. With new internals, they at least would get  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712720,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a chance to break-even on hardware - and this \ninvestment piled onto the division’s burn rate. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717600,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 2005, Microsoft would enter its second era \n- a golden age for companies and consumers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722800,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that would last for the next 8 years. The Xbox \nwould evolve from a promising challenger into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727920,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a disruptive market leader. By throwing in the \ntowel early on the prior generation, Microsoft  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733040,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "beat Sony to market, releasing the Xbox 360 a \nyear ahead of the PS3. This period would be the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739280,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "peak of the console wars and when Microsoft would \nfinally turn a profit from their gaming venture.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744320,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Despite being launched a year prior, Halo 2 was an \nindustry-defining, mass-market blockbuster whose  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750080,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "momentum directly carried over to the second \ngeneration. The game propelled the 360 and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755440,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Xbox Live as essentials for mainstream audiences. \nFor Microsoft, the future was online and the 360  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761440,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was built to accelerate that. The Xbox Dashboard \nwas redesigned into a gamified operating system  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with tabs, Achievements, Gamer Score, and digital \nstorefronts. Players could download demos or buy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772720,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "minigames like Geometry Wars on Xbox Live Arcade. \nWith unified shader architecture, shared memory,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777280,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and multi-core processing, the Xbox 360 pioneered \nengineering standards and visuals that no other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782480,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "console on the market could replicate. Microsoft \nhad learned enough from its prior era to keep  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787360,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "games flowing and paid off studios to ensure their \ngames would come to Xbox first. In the first year,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 792320,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there was Call of Duty 2 with HD resolution and \nvolumetric smoke, Elder Scrolls Oblivion with HDR  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798240,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lighting and a massive fully interactive world, \nGhost Recon, which could render two viewpoints  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803040,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at once, and Gears of War, a genre-defining \ntitle for third-person cover shooters. Timed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808640,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exclusives like Dead Rising, Bioshock, and Mass \nEffect only further solidified the 360’s winning  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814400,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "position. All these games helped propel Xbox \nLive to record subscribers. Yet all this success  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819440,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from being first-to-market would come at a cost.\nMicrosoft had paid IBM and ATI to develop custom  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825440,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "CPUs and GPUs for the 360. They took a page from \nNintendo and kept the blueprints for themselves.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830640,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But to beat Sony to market and improve on \nthe unit economics, Microsoft cut corners  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to accelerate production and lower costs. They \nput the GPU directly underneath the disc drive,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840240,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the hottest part of the console, opted for cheap \naluminum to cool the GPU, and used clips over  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845440,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "screws to secure the chip to the motherboard. \nWith inadequate cooling, the 360 would overheat,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851040,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the clips that held the GPU would snap off as \nthe motherboard warped, and the console would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855680,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "die. Microsoft knew during production that this \nwas a problem as up to 7 out of every 10 consoles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861280,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "failed off the factory line months before launch. \nYet company executives still stuck to the release  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866160,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "date. The Red Ring of Death was a global epidemic. \nMillions of consoles failed worldwide, even after  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871920,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multiple repairs and replacements. It was only \nafter scrutiny from the mainstream media that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876880,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Microsoft finally admitted that the root cause was \ndesign, not user error. To protect the upcoming  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882640,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Halo 3 launch and console reputation, Ballmer \ncommitted over $1 billion in cash on a proactive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888400,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass recall. They covered overnight shipping, \nextended all warranties to 3 years, retroactively  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894160,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reimbursed repair fees, and swapped motherboards \nfor free around the world. This crisis would have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899280,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been fatal had it not been for Sony’s blunders \nwith the PS3. As a result, Microsoft was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904400,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "allowed to outrun a hardware catastrophe that \nwould have wiped them out in any other era. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908880,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Microsoft could not bribe every single studio in \nthe world to only make games for the 360. They  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914880,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were saved by the reality that most developers \nliterally could not deal with Playstation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919840,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Development on the PS3 between 2005 and 2008 \nwas a technical nightmare, so much so that most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926400,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "developers preferred to develop for the 360, even \nif the underlying console was broken. Sony had  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931680,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "initially entered this era as the king. The gaming \ndivision was led by Ken Kutaragi, the Father of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937040,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the PlayStation. While Windows was the cash cow \nthat subsidized Xbox at Microsoft, Playstation was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 943040,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the star at Sony. Despite being late to the party, \nSony still held the advantage in player base,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948720,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first-party franchises, and developer support. \nNo matter how great the 360 was, most PS2  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "players were willing to wait for the PS3. Kutaragi \nbelieved that the Playstation should be elevated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958640,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into a supercomputer with innovation to last \na decade. The PS3 boasted twice as much power,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964720,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "played Blu-Ray in an era when high definition DVD \nwas just taking off, and cost $200 more than the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970880,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Xbox 360. At launch, Kutaragi proclaimed that the \nPS3 was not a game machine and that he expected  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 977680,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "consumers to simply work more to afford the price \njump. But the hardware was so complicated that it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983040,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hindered first-party studios and alienated \nthird-party developers. Cross-platform games  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988400,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like Madden, Tony Hawk, and Splinter Cell looked \nand performed far worse on the PS3 than on the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 994000,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "360. Developers had to downscale textures, lower \nframerate, remove shadows, and gut lighting just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 999200,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to get their titles working on the Playstation. \nIt was the same for exclusives like Haze, Genji,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004400,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Heavenly Sword. There were a few \ntitles like Resistance, Ratchet \u0026 Clank,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008720,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and MGS4 that nailed the PS3, but these were \nthe exception rather than the norm. As a result,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1014800,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most developers stayed with the 360. Games that \nwere meant to be on PS3 organically turned into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1020320,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Xbox exclusives during the Red Ring of Death.\nXbox had escaped disaster thanks to Sony’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1025520,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "self-destruction. With gamers reassured, \nthe hardware on a clear path to recovery,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1029920,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the launch of Halo 3, 2008 was a pivotal year \nfor Xbox. It would be the first time in history  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1036160,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the gaming division would turn a profit, \nmarking the accounting success of a decade-long,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1040400,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multi-billion dollar bet. At the same time, the \n360 dashboard was revamped to be more like the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1045600,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nintendo Wii, evolving from its cold, 2D aesthetic \ninto a softer, whimsical, gradient, 3D interface  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1051360,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for casuals. The irony was not lost on Microsoft’s \nleaders that Xbox’s best business had come in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1057120,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Great Recession. They had slashed 360 prices \nto pressure the PS3, so the money couldn’t have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1062560,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "come from console sales. Instead, the profit \nwas coming from subscriptions, royalties,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1067280,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the now blossoming Xbox Live Arcade. Americans \nstopped shopping, going to the movies, and taking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1073200,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vacations, but they still played video games \ndespite the decline in retail and employment. They  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1078000,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simply traded down to $10 indie exclusives on the \nXbox Live Arcade like Braid and Castle Crashers.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1083920,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The blowout success of The Summer of Arcade \nrevealed to Microsoft a revenue stream that would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1088400,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prove to be far more lucrative than subscriptions \nor one-time purchases. Transactional revenue,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1093600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like downloadable indie games, expansions, \nmap packs, and avatar accessories, had much  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1098720,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "higher profit margins than the conventional $60 \ndiscs that required manufacturing, shipping,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1103920,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and a cut to retailers. And unlike CDs, \ndigital downloads were not transferable.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1109040,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was a lock-in deeper than any friends list \nand a direct extension of platform-level LTV,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1114560,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rather than franchise-LTV. The more online \ntransactions, the greater the digital library,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1119760,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the lower the churn. As Xbox Live Arcade grew \nin popularity, indie developers started grossing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1125680,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "millions of dollars without touching a single \nCD. The better margins and growing consumer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1130480,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "acceptance towards digital distribution became too \ncompelling for major publishers and Microsoft to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1135920,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ignore. By 2010, both sides had united, bringing \nfull games onto Xbox Live for direct download at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1141760,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same price as retail. For every third-party \ndigital sale, Microsoft’s royalty jumped from 10  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1147280,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to 30% and the publishers’ takes leaped from \n50 to 70%. By 2011, Microsoft was generating  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1154080,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more revenue from online transactions than \nfrom Xbox Live subscriptions. It was a clone  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1159040,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the Apple App Store - just applied to gamers.\nWith studios and publishers hitting their stride  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1164480,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with blockbusters like Modern Warfare, Left \n4 Dead, Red Dead Redemption, and Assassin’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1169280,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Creed 2, the Xbox brand and business was \nat an all time high. Microsoft dismissed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1173840,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all talk of a successor console and internally \nmandated that the Xbox experience be baked into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1179200,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Windows - rather than the other way around. The \n360 at this point was 6 years old. It dominated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1184960,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "headlines and North America, but would ultimately \nend this generation as a close second to the PS3. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1191360,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The internet is a market, not an art gallery. \nOnline businesses are brutally competitive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1195920,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where any bit of success attracts rivals and \nimitators. Content is no different - on YouTube,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1201280,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every minute someone spends watching another \nchannel is a minute not spent on Modern MBA. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1206000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The growing saturation in business content creates \nnew challenges. Views can be easily botted,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1210960,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "subscriber counts no longer matter, and \nwe have to know what the competition is up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1214640,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to, what formats or topics are getting traction, \nand whether we’re falling behind. Audience  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1217200,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "demand is finite and being the first to a trend \nmatters more than making the 1000th video on why  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1222880,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Subway failed. And when we have plenty of data on \nYouTube, we get no visibility into how Modern MBA  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1228960,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "content spreads across the rest of the Internet.\nThat's where Accio has completely changed the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1233520,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "game for us. Everyone talks about AI agents but \nunless you go weeding through SDKs, API Keys,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1239120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and access tokens, it’s all marketing. Claude \nand ChatGPT can’t even open links. There’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1244320,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no easy way to build an agent that executes \nexactly what you want until now with Accio. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1249440,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With Accio Work, we created one single agent \nin just 15 minutes by simply explaining what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1254560,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we need in simple English - no code or APIs. We \ngave our agent three tasks, one for each of our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1259120,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "business challenges. The first task is tracking \ndistribution, every 24 hours, it crawls the web  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1264560,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on its own looking for any and every mention of \nModern MBA outside of YouTube in the past 2 weeks.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1270720,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then it gives us a daily digest summarizing \nwhat videos are spreading across English and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1274960,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "non-English markets and how people are reacting. \nBefore Accio, we tried to manually monitor every  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1280640,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "platform, region, and language, but it would \ntake hours of endless searching to collect just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1285120,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a fraction of these results. And when we tried to \nask ChatGPT or Claude, it would just spit out the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1290560,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same outdated results from their training data.\nOur Accio agent also handles competitive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1295520,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "intelligence. It monitors channels similar to \nModern MBA and summarizes their top-performing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1300960,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "videos, while tracking any changes in their upload \nfrequency, thumbnails, titles, or format. What’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1306880,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "awesome is that our agent looks beyond just the \nvideo, it tracks if these channels are launching  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1311440,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "merchandise, selling courses, paywalling content, \nand shifting their business model. Instead of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1316800,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "manually auditing dozens of competitors weekly, \nwe get a daily snapshot of our entire landscape.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1322240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The third task is topics. Every day, our agent \nscans the Internet for the latest companies and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1327680,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stories that are gaining momentum in the news \ncycle. But instead of dumping a list, our agent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1332320,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proactively checks against Modern MBA’s existing \ninventory to see if we’ve already made a video, if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1336000,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "similar channels have already tackled the \ntopic, and how those videos have performed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1340000,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to determine if there’s any demand left for \nthe topic. Now with Accio, timing can be a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1344560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strategy rather than just algorithmic luck.\nWe’ve configured our agent to run every 12-24  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1349680,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hours and to maintain memory, track changes \nover time, and filter for duplicates. At the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1355200,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "end of the day, you have to understand the market \nyou’re in. You don’t need to copy the competition,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1359440,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but you can’t operate in a vacuum either. People \ndon’t consume in isolation and businesses don’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1363680,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "compete in isolation. If you’re not \ntracking where the demand is going,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1367120,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’re flying blind. Thanks to Accio Work, we \ncan now monitor competitors systematically,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1372160,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "identify opportunities, and understand trends \nacross our vertical without wasting hundreds of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1376720,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hours manually researching everything ourselves.\nAI tools like ChatGPT, Claude, or Perplexity are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1381360,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "general-purpose chatbots built for \nthe mass-market. They help you think,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1384800,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but don’t execute. They can’t even open links \nand you have to repeatedly type in the same  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1388880,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prompts and context to remind them what you’re \nlooking for. Accio Work is different. It’s not a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1394240,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chatbot - it’s a specialized AI revenue-generating \nengine built from the ground up for business  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1399200,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "owners and entrepreneurs. Trained on decades of \nAlibaba’s proprietary data, Accio Work doesn’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1404880,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just talk - it executes and deploys agents to do \nthe heavy lifting. Download Accio Work for Desktop  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1411040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "using the link in the description to unlock \nbonus credits exclusive to Modern MBA viewers. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1416320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks again to Accio for supporting Modern \nMBA and making this episode possible. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1424320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At this point in the early 2010s, Sony had \nrecovered. The PS3’s disastrous debut had forced  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1430000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a reset in leadership, pricing, and marketing. \nBack in Japan, Kutaragi was demoted. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1435520,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Playstation team was no longer allowed to operate \nindependently and required to collaborate with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1440240,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other divisions. Sony appointed Kaz Hirai, who \nhad shepherded PS2 in the prior era, as the new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1446320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leader of Playstation. Under Kaz, Sony would spend \nthe next 2 years with its head down restructuring  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1451920,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the organization and redesigning the console - \nwhich gave Xbox room to shrug off the Red Ring  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1457040,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of Death. Sony shrunk the processors, cut costs, \nand revamped developer tooling. At the same time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1463920,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their internal studios like Naughty Dog had begun \nto master the PS3 architecture and started sharing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1469520,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their techniques and knowledge with the technical \ncommunity. The tides shifted with the launch of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1475120,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the $299 PS3 Slim in 2009. Backed by Uncharted \n2, Demon’s Soul, Killzone 2, God of War 3,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1483200,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gran Turismo 5, and Heavy Rain, these exclusives \neclipsed Xbox games in visuals, lighting, physics,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1489840,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and scale. By 2013, the PS3 had surpassed the \n360 in sales. But despite losing in hardware,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1496880,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Microsoft still came out ahead in profits. The 360 \nwas a far more effective and efficient business  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1502320,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with subscriptions, transactional revenue, \nand digital stores - income streams that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1507280,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Playstation would not tap into until the next era.\nBut for all the fortunes that the gaming division  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1512320,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was bringing in, Ballmer needed more. He pushed \nthat the Xbox could and should be more than a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1517440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "console for gamers. There were two realities he \nwas contending with. The first was external. While  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1522960,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Xbox and Sony were duking it out, the real winner \nof this generation was Nintendo. The Wii outsold  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1528800,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both the Xbox 360 and the PS3 by tens of millions \nof units. Nintendo knew it could never compete on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1535120,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "specs or match the warchests of Microsoft and \nSony. To fight both these giants over the same  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1540160,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pool of hardcore gamers was suicide. Satoru Iwata, \nthe president of Nintendo, mandated the company  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1546240,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to focus on gameplay and unique controls over \nchips, graphics, and processing power. The DS  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1552080,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was a resounding success - combining established \ntechnologies like dual screens, resistive touch,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1557760,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "styluses, and microphones in new ways to elevate \ngameplay. It was more profitable and outsold the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1563680,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "PSP by 2 to 1. The Wii was a continuation of \nthis strategy. Designed with motion-control and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1569840,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "positioned as a self-improvement appliance, the \nWii was both a breakout cultural and commercial  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1574240,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hit. At the peak of the console wars, Nintendo \nwas by far the most profitable of the three,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1579280,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "selling consoles and games at the highest marg \nins. But even they eventually stumbled. By 2011,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1585200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "smartphones had become mainstream devices. \nThe non-gamers migrated to Angry Birds and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1590080,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fruit Ninja. The novelty of the Wii had worn \noff, the console was so underpowered that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1595040,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cross-platform blockbusters that people did want \nto play were watered-down ports, and third-party  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1600080,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "developers felt overshadowed by Nintendo’s \nfirst-party titles. Nintendo’s reign came crashing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1605280,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "down in 2012 with the first ever operating loss \nin company history. These losses would continue  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1610640,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for three consecutive years. Not even the 3DS \nand Wii U could stand against iPhones, iPads,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1616080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Androids. By 2014, investors and analysts \nwere heckling for Nintendo to give up on consoles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1621600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entirely and make Mario games for smartphones.\nEven though the Wii didn’t stay on top, it was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1626640,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "still considered the most innovative console of \nthis generation. Nintendo had demonstrated with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1631680,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the right technology and application, consoles \ncould be more than just toys. The market rewarded  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1636880,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them for mass-market innovation. At the peak \nof Wii, Nintendo’s stock had skyrocketed by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1642320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "400% to become the most valuable company in all of \nJapan, ahead of Canon and behind only Toyota - an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1647920,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unprecedented milestone for a video game company. \nIn contrast, as much as Sony and Xbox pushed the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1653760,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "envelope in both for hardcore gamers, they \nreceived little recognition from investors.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1658160,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sony’s stock was hammered throughout the PS3 era. \nThe company lost billions in yen every year until  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1663840,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kaz’s turnaround. Meanwhile, Microsoft’s stock \nhad stagnated under Ballmer for over a decade.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1669440,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While the 360 was a massive story inside the \nindustry, outside, the Xbox was still perceived  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1675040,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a niche toy for teens and nerds. With the \nexception of the Wii, Wall Street viewed gaming  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1679840,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a risky hit-driven business, no different from \nHollywood movie studios. It was either boom or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1685600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bust and any growth would get wiped out with every \nnew generation of consoles. For all the billions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1690640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that Xbox generated, their success paled in \ncomparison to the exponential growth of Apple and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1695920,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Google. Ballmer had delivered bigger numbers every \nyear, but was under intense pressure to deliver  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1700800,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ground-breaking, mass-market breakthroughs \nthat Microsoft’s rivals had achieved. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1705360,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Compared to FANG, Microsoft was a dinosaur. They \nhad money but no strategy or cool factor. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1710960,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "company had been late to every major tech shift of \nthe last decade. They missed out on portable media  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1715840,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "players and search in the mid-2000s and e-books, \nsmartphones, streaming, cloud infrastructure in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1721200,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the late 2000s. The Zune was 5 years late and \nhad no shot against the iTunes and iPod. Bing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1726720,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "derived its miniscule market share by being forced \nonto Windows and Yahoo users. Ballmer famously  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1732240,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "laughed at the iPhone, only to panic years later \nlaunching the Windows Phone and acquiring Nokia  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1737440,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "after Android and Apple had already conquered \nthe market. The same thing happened with MSN and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1742480,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Facebook, Internet Explorer and Chrome, Surface \nand iPad, Microsoft Office and Google Docs, Skype  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1746880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and WhatsApp. Under Ballmer, Microsoft was \nincreasingly irrelevant in consumer hardware and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1752080,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "software, forced to release these me-too products \nto stop their rivals from taking everything. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1757440,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the late 2000s, the pressure on Ballmer was at \nboiling point. Investors publicly called for his  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1762800,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "removal. Under fire, Ballmer poured all his hopes \nonto the gaming division. It was the company’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1767840,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only winning horse that wasn’t Windows or Office. \nWith an established audience, strong ecosystem,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1773600,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and industry-leading momentum, the Xbox 360 \nwas the perfect vehicle for some kind of bold,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1779520,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "game-changing hardware innovation. The success \nof the Wii and iPhone strengthened Ballmer’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1784800,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "obsession with natural user interfaces \nas Microsoft’s ticket back to the top.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1789520,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He believed that through Xbox, Microsoft \ncould lead this new world free of mice,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1793840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "keyboards, screens, one living room at a \ntime. The Kinect was the culmination of all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1798880,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these efforts - an all-or-nothing gamble to save \nBallmer’s job, deliver an exciting, mass-market  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1803760,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "device, broaden appeal beyond hardcore gamers, and \nre-energize Microsoft in the eyes of shareholders.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1810320,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With Kinect, you became the controller. You \ncould wave your hand to navigate the dashboard,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1814960,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "issue voice commands without touching a \nmicrophone, sign in with facial recognition,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1819360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so on. The futuristic premise of a remote-free \nliving room made Kinect the fastest-selling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1824560,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "consumer electronics device in history, ahead of \nthe iPhone and iPad. Microsoft quickly reframed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1830240,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Xbox as an all-in-one entertainment device \nto give their new casual audience something to do  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1835360,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "beyond dancing and gaming - starting with the most \ntimeless living room activity of all: watching TV. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1841520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Microsoft redesigned the Xbox dashboard to push \nthis new positioning, replacing the classic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1846640,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "glossy, whimsical gamer interface in favor of the \nflat, minimalist, tiles from Windows. By dulling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1852800,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the interface, users would be drawn to the few \ntiles with color - which were by design the games,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1858240,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "streaming apps, and advertisements. The intent \nwas obvious - get users out of the dashboard  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1862960,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and funnel all idle engagement into content. Media \ncompanies all raced to become first-class citizens  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1868480,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the new Xbox dashboard. By 2012, a third of \n360 owners had bought Kinect and over 100 cable,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1875520,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sports, and entertainment channels were integrated \ninto the home page. Microsoft proudly proclaimed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1880560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that for the first time ever, the average Xbox \nLive user was spending more time watching content  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1885680,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "instead of playing games. This reaffirming \nuser data, Kinect’s record-breaking sales,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1890880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the commercial success of the overall gaming \nbusiness gave Ballmer and Xbox’s leaders the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1896080,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "illusion that they had finally achieved \nthat elusive mass- market breakthrough. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1899920,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The reality was that Microsoft’s executives, like \nmost in the corporate world, ignored history and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1905040,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "used data to see what they wanted to see. Kinect \nsold well solely on the basis of being a new,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1910480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exciting gadget. Like the Wii, once the novelty \nwore off, most devices just collected dust.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1916240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Third-party development was also limited. It was \ndifficult to design for the imprecise gestures,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1921680,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "input lag, and inevitable player fatigue. The \ndata cited by leadership was also a classic case  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1927200,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of survivorship bias. It only captured the \nbehavior of the 360 owners that were online  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1932560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rather than the vast majority that was offline. \nUltimately, people watched Netflix, YouTube,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1937680,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and movies on their Xbox because it was already \nplugged in - not because they didn’t care about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1942160,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "games. At the same time, the numbers between 2010 \nand 2012 for the overall Xbox business were truly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1948480,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spectacular for a 7-year-old console. Microsoft \nincorrectly attributed this performance to Kinect,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1954160,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the new audience of casuals, and increased \ncontent consumption. But this was correlation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1959280,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not causation. In reality, Xbox’s success \nin this period had little to do with TV and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1964720,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kinect. Instead, most of the money came from \nthe platform’s biggest franchises who were all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1969440,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "releasing their finales. Halo Reach, Gears of War \n3, and Fable 3 all came out in this period. From  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1975680,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "another lens, it was understandable why Microsoft \nwould be so quick to drink its own Kool-Aid. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1980480,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Xbox team had a stellar track record in reshaping \nthe industry - they had redefined online gaming  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1985600,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into a centralized subscription service, pioneered \ndigital distribution, and built a platform  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1990560,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for indie developers years before anyone else.\nFrom 2013 to 2019, Microsoft would enter its third  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1997440,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "era with the fatal misunderstanding that people no \nlonger wanted consoles to play games anymore. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2002640,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mistake would be the beginning of the end for both \nXbox and Steve Ballmer. The long-awaited Xbox One  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2008480,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reveal was a disaster. Priced at $500, the console \ncame with a slew of radical requirements. Kinect  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2014960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was now mandatory and had to be connected at all \ntimes for the console to even turn on. Second,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2020240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Xbox would check Internet connection every 24 \nhours. If the console was not online, users would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2025680,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be blocked from playing games, even offline \nsingle-player titles, until the Internet was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2030320,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "restored. The classic CD was now just a license. \nEvery game had to be fully installed to the hard  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2036640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "drive and tied to one Xbox account. Players could \nno longer share discs with friends or buy used  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2041920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "games. And with region lock, games of one region \ncould only be played on consoles that had been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2046960,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "registered in that same country. To make matters \nworse, the reveal was more about TV than games.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2052800,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The big announcements were a partnership with the \nNFL, the promise of a Steve Spielberg-produced  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2057360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "live action Halo TV show, a new network where \nMicrosoft would produce and broadcast its own  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2062800,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exclusive programming, and TV-specific voice \ncommands for Kinect. These were all business  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2067760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decisions to normalize digital distribution, \nmaximize transactional revenue from casuals, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2073040,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "optimize engagement for advertisers. The blowback \nwas intense - and Sony quickly capitalized on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2078880,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their rival’s self-destruction. Just one week \nafter the Xbox One reveal, Sony announced  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2084000,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to a standing ovation that the PS4 supported \nused games, required no Internet connection to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2089040,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "play offline games, and would cost $100 less than \nthe Xbox One. Microsoft hastily abandoned these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2095280,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "policies a month later before the console was \neven released, but the damage had already been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2100080,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "done. All the investment had gone into this TV \nfuture, rather than games. With four times the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2105360,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "amount of exclusives on Playstation than on the \nXbox, the PS4 would go on to outsell the Xbox One  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2111120,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2:1 for the remainder of the decade. Microsoft \nhad lost the generation before it even started. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2116640,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The disastrous Xbone keynote, combined with the \nNokia acquisition, proved fatal for Ballmer. Satya  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2122400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nadella was appointed with a mandate to usher \nin change. Investors demanded for a reset - they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2127520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "called for an end to the billions wasted chasing \nApple and Google, a ruthless optimization of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2132480,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Windows and Office cash cow, a singular focus \non Azure cloud for growth, and a spin-off of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2137680,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Xbox. Satya was faced with two choices - he could \neither sterilize Microsoft into a safe but boring  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2143680,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enterprise relic like IBM or take another stab at \nbringing the company back to the forefront with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2148800,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exciting consumer products and strong enterprise \nbusinesses. The former would mean a slow walk to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2152240,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the graveyard while the latter would be another \nhigh-stakes gamble to modernize the legacy giant.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2156560,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ultimately, the best way to grow the stock was not \nby adding a few hundred million more in revenue or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2161360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "issuing greater dividends - Ballmer had already \ntried that for a decade. Instead, Satya had to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2166560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "win the optics. Investors had to see the company \nas a growth stock like FANG and keeping Xbox was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2172320,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "key to maintaining relevance amongst consumers. \nTo demonstrate that Microsoft would no longer be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2177840,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same greedy, out-of-touch dinosaur, Satya \ndid in his first year what Ballmer had refused  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2183280,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to do for decades - releasing Microsoft Office \nfor iPad, open sourcing .NET, taking a photo  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2189200,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in front of a Linux sign, and releasing Visual \nStudio for free. He gave Xbox the same treatment,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2194960,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "firing the TV execs and appointing an Xbox lifer \nin their place as a show of goodwill to gamers.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2201200,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Satya had made his career at the company scaling \nAzure and he pushed Microsoft to embrace a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2206000,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "software-first business. Microsoft had been losing \nthe war in hardware for generations and to him,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2211600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "consumer devices like gaming consoles were \nhigh-risk, low-margin, and near impossible to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2216960,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get right. Instead, the real financial engine \nof FANG and the biggest tech startups was in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2222520,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "software-as-a-service. Apple’s true business was \nnot in the glass and aluminium of the iPhone,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2226640,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but rather in the recurring revenues of the App \nStore and iCloud - and Satya mandated Microsoft  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2231440,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could do the same across consumer and enterprise.\nDespite Phil Spencer’s public efforts, the Xbox  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2236880,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One had fallen so far behind in 2017 that it \nwas clear that Microsoft would never catch up to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2242560,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Playstation. With the launch of Xbox Live Anywhere \nand GamePass, the gaming division embraced Satya’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2248240,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cloud-first, platform-agnostic SaaS endgame. They \nstopped caring what devices people played their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2253360,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "games on so long as they owned the user data and \nmonetization. Engagement, rather than units sold,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2258960,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "became the new defining metric for all of Xbox. \nUnder this model, even first-party flops like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2264560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Halo 4 and 5 and Gears of War 4 and 5 were all \nframed as major successes for their engagement  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2270320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rather than gameplay or reception. On the other \nhand, GamePass was a direct copy of the Netflix  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2275920,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "playbook. The price was aggressively subsidized \nand the catalog was so stacked that it was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2280480,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impossible for players to not sign up. At the same \ntime, Microsoft poured capital into anything that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2284880,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could drive engagement, acquiring studios like \nMojang, Obsidian, Double Fine, and Ninja Theory  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2290560,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on top of a live streaming platform. Success was \nno longer an endless race to deliver the next hit,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2295920,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but instead about growing digital engagement and \nadding enough content every quarter to collect  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2300320,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rent. By late 2018, Microsoft only reported \n2 metrics for its gaming business - MAU and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2306800,
    "dDurationMs": 1239,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gameplay hours. Xbox’s moves in this era pushed \nthe gaming industry to where it is today where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2308039,
    "dDurationMs": 601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most games are live services that exist only \nto sustain engagement and retention. Thus,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2308640,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even as consumer reception to first-party titles \ndeclined, Microsoft no longer had a reason to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2314080,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "care about how players felt about the games, so \nlong as they stayed engaged. The Xbox division  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2319280,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surpassed a record $10B in revenue, despite \nbeing completely crushed in hardware by the PS4. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2325440,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even though the PS4 crushed the Xbone in volume, \nthe Playstation as a business was nowhere as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2328880,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "effective or efficient. From the standpoint of \ngamers, Sony had done everything right - they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2333600,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "double-downed on single-player titles in a \ntime when the industry chased multiplayer,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2337840,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "funded high-budget exclusives like BloodBorne \nand Spiderman, and paid for timed exclusives  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2342720,
    "dDurationMs": 5502,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on third-party blockbusters to cement Playstation \nas the only home for gamers. While Microsoft went  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2348222,
    "dDurationMs": 3858,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on a buying spree picking up studios right and \nleft in the pursuit of quantity, Sony’s internal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2352080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "studios were given progressively longer runways \nand larger budgets to deliver quality. The company  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2357680,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "treated first-party exclusives like timeless art \nand believed that bundling them into subscriptions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2362640,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would devalue their worth. Ultimately, it was a \ncase of opposites - Microsoft was extracting far  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2368080,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "higher spend and margins from its smaller \naudience under subscriptions and services  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2372560,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than Sony was with its much larger customer base \nunder the old-school hardware and ownership model. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2378160,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 2020, the Xbox entered its fourth and \npresent-day era where the chickens have come home  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2383040,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to roost. To drive software revenue and devalue \nhardware, Microsoft spent $69B to buy Activision  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2389360,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Blizzard. With 240 million monthly Candy Crush \nplayers and a controlling stake in mobile gaming,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2395040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Microsoft could now monetize the users on the \ndevices of its biggest rivals. And as the new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2400240,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "owner of Call of Duty, Microsoft now owns the \nmost-played game on Playstation and pockets  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2405360,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "70% of every microtransaction that occurs on \nSony hardware. However, now that the cash has  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2411200,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been spent and interest rates have climbed, \nSatya is under pressure to return that $69B  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2417120,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to Microsoft shareholders. In the prior era, the \nlow interest rates and SaaS gold rush enabled the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2422240,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Xbox division to focus on growth at all costs, \nwhere GamePass subscriptions were given away for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2427440,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "free and dozens of studios were funded to make \nsomething for everyone. Meanwhile, the hardware  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2432240,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strategy was allowed to completely wither away.\nThese days, the Xbox is a ghost on Microsoft’s own  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2438080,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "storefronts and properties. The sales gap between \nit and the PS5 today have widened to a staggering  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2443520,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ratio of 3:1. There are simply no longer enough \nXbox owners for Microsoft to sell first-party  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2449440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exclusives to. This reality has forced a radical \npivot where iconic franchises that once defined  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2454960,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Xbox experience like Halo, Forza, and Gears \nof War are now being ported to the Playstation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2460800,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "under the cold logic that any money in software is \ngood money. With console sales and consumer trust  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2466400,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the dirt, the entire Xbox business today \nrides on GamePass. To extract maximum value,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2472160,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Microsoft had pushed price hikes and membership \ntiers. Like Netflix, Satya’s entire bet hinges  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2477200,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the belief that subscription in itself is a \nmoat, that people won’t churn due to convenience,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2482000,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that when you own most of the content in the \nfirst place, it really doesn’t matter how much  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2486240,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of it is slop. At the same time, every internal \nstudio has been placed under a mandate to achieve  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2491520,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "30% profit margins, creatives who stuck around \na under the promise of autonomy have all been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2496320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pushed out, perennial underperformers like \n343 Industries have been promoted for their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2501040,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "obedience and ability to be franchise factories, \nand award-winning developers like Tango and Arkane  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2506480,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have been shuttered as cultural and economic \nliabilities. Xbox today stands for neither  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2511360,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "good hardware nor good games - only liquidation.\nUltimately, Satya was successful in his grand goal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2517440,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of transforming how investors perceived Microsoft. \nUnder him, Microsoft’s stock has consistently  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2523200,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "traded at double the P/E ratio of when the company \nwas run by Ballmer. Ironically, Satya promised  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2529120,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at his appointment to usher in a new culture of \nopen source, empathy, and product excellence over  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2534000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the closed, clinical value extraction of the 90s. \nOver his tenure, he did enough on the PR front to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2540160,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "appoint the right talking heads for Xbox - leaders \nwho wore the T-shirts, pounded their chests,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2545040,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and said all the right things to appease gamers \nand regulators. But now that Satya has won over  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2550160,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "investors, he no longer needs to curry favor \nwith consumers - and Xbox’s leaders have all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2555200,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been swiftly replaced with yes-men and yes-women \nwho will execute on his profit mandates and have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2560720,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no loyalty to anything beyond their own careers. \nIn hindsight, while Ballmer deserves plenty of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2566240,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "criticism for the Xbone - at least he was trying \nto grow the pie. Today, it’s the worst of all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2570960,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "worlds, where engagement is more important than \nquality, talent is expendable, and products are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2576000,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "built to kill time rather than excite consumers. \nHardware is for suckers and risk-taking is for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2581440,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "losers. Under this mindset, the original Xbox \nwould have never existed. Despite a decade talking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2586720,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up Microsoft as being a software-first company, \nits other consumer products—Windows, Outlook,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2593120,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "365, Surface—have followed the same path as \nXbox as hollow, rent-seeking subscriptions. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2599440,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s bad to generalize, but the other angle \nis that gamers have repeatedly proven to be a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2604000,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forgiving audience with short memory - quick to \noutrage but also easy to placate and win back.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2609760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Xbox fanboys are now shouting from their soapboxes \nthat the console wars are back and Asha Sharma  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2614640,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the right leader to return the console to \nits glory days. She lowered GamePass prices,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2619680,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "removed Copilot from the dashboard, renewed \na commitment to platform exclusives,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2623920,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and handed out free consoles to conference \nattendees. The reality is that these moves  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2628480,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are just symbolic half-measures designed to \nsoften the impending layoffs and budget-cuts  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2632240,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that are about to sweep over the entire division.\nIn the words of her own memo, Xbox today operates  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2637200,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at an unacceptable 3% profit margin. The entire \nbusiness is currently being kept afloat by Call of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2643120,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Duty slop and Candy Crush microtransactions. The \nreality is that Asha’s own tenure, compensation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2648000,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and career won’t be decided by Twitter \nheadlines and player sentiment. Instead,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2654000,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "her performance review and job security rides \nentirely on one thing - how quickly can she lift  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2658000,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Xbox business out of this 3% margin and how \nhigh can she drive profits using the least amount  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2663120,
    "dDurationMs": 14480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of investment. From this lens, her promise to \nrestore Xbox as a leader is pure fantasy. There is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2677600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no way to develop quality first-party exclusives, \nfoster a thriving third-party ecosystem,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2682640,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or push the boundary in hardware while the \nunderlying division is being slashed to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2686720,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the bone. All of these efforts, if properly \npursued, require time, capital, expertise,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2693360,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "talent, and passion - things that Xbox \nhas lost over the past decade and can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2696880,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no longer afford. What Asha is doing today is \nreactive. She’s picking the low-hanging fruit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2703680,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that requires no conviction, no vision, and no \nrisk. Appeasing the masses is not a strategy. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2709840,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The fall of Xbox is the best modern day \nreminder of the importance of competition  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2714240,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that monetization is no replacement \nfor product. The console wars of the 2010s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2719280,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were not just entertainment for gamers, but a \npressure cooker that compelled innovation. When  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2724000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sony fumbled the PS3, when Microsoft botched the \n360, and when Nintendo messed up with the Wii U,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2730160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they were each forced to redeem themselves through \ninnovation - and in doing so, they gave consumers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2735120,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some of the best titles, features, experiences, \nand hardware in gaming history. Today, the urgency  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2741120,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and incentive for innovation rides only on the \npersonal values of every company’s leaders."
    } ]
  } ]
}
